[{"title": "", "html": "<p>These pages set out, side by side, how Koine Greek and Latin work: the letters and sounds, the one principle that drives both languages, and the systems of nouns, pronouns and verbs. They follow the poster <i>Koine Greek &amp; Latin: A Side-by-Side 101</i>. The marks = ≈ ≠ say whether the two languages work the same way, nearly the same way, or differently.</p>"}, {"title": "Overview: two languages, one sentence", "html": "<p>A Side-by-Side 101 · From First Principles</p>\n<p class=\"jc-sub\"><i>Two alphabets, one ancient grammar — the letters, the sounds, and a four-year course in outline</i></p>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The two languages</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>ἡ κοινή · “the common” Greek</th><th>LINGVA LATINA · Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>c. 300 BC–AD 300. Spread by Alexander’s conquests, it became the everyday language of the eastern Mediterranean: the Septuagint, the New Testament, Polybius, Epictetus.</td><td>Classical c. 100 BC–AD 200: Cicero, Caesar, Virgil. As Church and scholarly Latin it lasted 1,500 more years: the Vulgate, Augustine, Aquinas, Newton.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>Cousins, not parent and child.</i></b> <i>Both descend from Proto-Indo-European. Latin’s alphabet came from a western Greek alphabet by way of the Etruscans.</i></p></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Legend</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>=</b></td><td>Same idea</td></tr><tr><td><b>≈</b></td><td>Same job, different tools</td></tr><tr><td><b>≠</b></td><td>Different</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>In the tables, Greek is on the left and Latin on the right.</i></p></figure>\n<h3>One sentence, both languages: John 1:1</h3>\n<p class=\"jc-sub\"><i>The Greek original and Jerome’s Latin Vulgate, word for word.</i></p>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>John 1:1, word for word</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek</th><th>Greek grammar</th><th>Latin</th><th>Latin grammar</th><th>English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Ἐν</b></td><td>preposition + <b>dative</b></td><td>In</td><td>preposition + <b>ablative</b></td><td>In</td></tr><tr><td><b>ἀρχῇ</b></td><td>noun · dative sg. fem. · 1st decl.</td><td>principiō</td><td>noun · ablative sg. neut. · 2nd decl.</td><td>the beginning</td></tr><tr><td><b>ἦν</b></td><td>εἰμί “be” · imperfect · 3rd sg.</td><td>erat</td><td>sum “be” · imperfect · 3rd sg.</td><td>was</td></tr><tr><td><b>ὁ λόγος</b></td><td>article + noun · nominative sg. masc.</td><td>Verbum</td><td>noun · nominative sg. neut. · no article</td><td>the Word</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Same skeleton:</b> preposition + case, the imperfect of “to be,” and a nominative subject placed after its verb.</li><li><b>Greek has “the” (ὁ); Latin has no articles at all.</b></li><li><b>Greek “in” takes the dative; Latin “in” takes the ablative</b> — Latin’s sixth case does work Greek gives the dative.</li><li><b>Gender can differ:</b> λόγος is masculine, verbum neuter.</li></ul>"}, {"title": "1 · Letters & Sounds", "html": "<div class=\"jc-row\"><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The Greek alphabet · 24 letters</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Name</th><th>Sound</th><th>As in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Α α</b></td><td>alpha</td><td>a</td><td>father</td></tr><tr><td><b>Β β</b></td><td>beta</td><td>b</td><td>boy</td></tr><tr><td><b>Γ γ</b></td><td>gamma</td><td>g</td><td>go · γγ = ng</td></tr><tr><td><b>Δ δ</b></td><td>delta</td><td>d</td><td>dog</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ε ε</b></td><td>epsilon</td><td>e</td><td>pet</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ζ ζ</b></td><td>zeta</td><td>z</td><td>zeal, “dz”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Η η</b></td><td>eta</td><td>ē</td><td>they</td></tr><tr><td><b>Θ θ</b></td><td>theta</td><td>th</td><td>thin</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ι ι</b></td><td>iota</td><td>i</td><td>machine, sit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Κ κ</b></td><td>kappa</td><td>k</td><td>kite</td></tr><tr><td><b>Λ λ</b></td><td>lambda</td><td>l</td><td>lamp</td></tr><tr><td><b>Μ μ</b></td><td>mu</td><td>m</td><td>mother</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ν ν</b></td><td>nu</td><td>n</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ξ ξ</b></td><td>xi</td><td>x</td><td>ax, “ks”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ο ο</b></td><td>omicron</td><td>o</td><td>ought (short)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Π π</b></td><td>pi</td><td>p</td><td>pet</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ρ ρ</b></td><td>rho</td><td>r</td><td>rolled r</td></tr><tr><td><b>Σ σ ς</b></td><td>sigma</td><td>s</td><td>sit · ς at end</td></tr><tr><td><b>Τ τ</b></td><td>tau</td><td>t</td><td>top</td></tr><tr><td><b>Υ υ</b></td><td>upsilon</td><td>u / y</td><td>French u</td></tr><tr><td><b>Φ φ</b></td><td>phi</td><td>ph</td><td>phone</td></tr><tr><td><b>Χ χ</b></td><td>chi</td><td>ch</td><td>loch, Bach</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ψ ψ</b></td><td>psi</td><td>ps</td><td>lips</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ω ω</b></td><td>omega</td><td>ō</td><td>tone (long)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>Sounds are the Erasmian system most classrooms use. Koine speakers were already shifting toward Modern Greek: η, ι, υ, ει, οι drifting toward “ee.”</i></p></figure><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The Latin alphabet · 23 letters</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Sound</th><th>As in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>A a</b></td><td>a</td><td>father, idea</td></tr><tr><td><b>B b</b></td><td>b</td><td>boy</td></tr><tr><td><b>C c</b></td><td>k</td><td>cat, always hard [ch before e, i]</td></tr><tr><td><b>D d</b></td><td>d</td><td>dog</td></tr><tr><td><b>E e</b></td><td>e</td><td>they, pet</td></tr><tr><td><b>F f</b></td><td>f</td><td>fan</td></tr><tr><td><b>G g</b></td><td>g</td><td>go, always hard [soft before e, i]</td></tr><tr><td><b>H h</b></td><td>h</td><td>hat</td></tr><tr><td><b>I i</b></td><td>i / y</td><td>machine; iam “yam”</td></tr><tr><td><b>K k</b></td><td>k</td><td>rare: Kalendae</td></tr><tr><td><b>L l</b></td><td>l</td><td>lamp</td></tr><tr><td><b>M m</b></td><td>m</td><td>mother</td></tr><tr><td><b>N n</b></td><td>n</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><b>O o</b></td><td>o</td><td>note, ought</td></tr><tr><td><b>P p</b></td><td>p</td><td>pet</td></tr><tr><td><b>Q q</b></td><td>kw</td><td>always qu</td></tr><tr><td><b>R r</b></td><td>r</td><td>rolled r</td></tr><tr><td><b>S s</b></td><td>s</td><td>sit, never z</td></tr><tr><td><b>T t</b></td><td>t</td><td>top</td></tr><tr><td><b>V v</b></td><td>u / w</td><td>rule; vīnum “wee-” [v]</td></tr><tr><td><b>X x</b></td><td>ks</td><td>ax</td></tr><tr><td><b>Y y</b></td><td>ü</td><td>Greek loans only</td></tr><tr><td><b>Z z</b></td><td>dz</td><td>Greek loans only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>J, U, W came later</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>Restored classical sounds, with church (ecclesiastical) Latin in brackets. No J, U, or W yet: I and V served as both vowel and consonant.</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>≈ Shared shapes.</i></b> <i>A B E Z H I K M N O T X Y look alike: Latin’s letters descend from Greek ones.</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>False friends.</i></b> <i>Greek H = long e · P = r · X = ch · Υ = ü · N ν = n · Latin C = k</i></p></figure></div>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Greek · beyond the letters</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Diphthongs</b></td><td>αι aisle · ει eight · οι oil · ου soup · αυ how · ευ eh-oo · υι we</td></tr><tr><td><b>Breathings</b></td><td>Every word-initial vowel (and initial ρ, written ῥ) carries one: rough ἁ = “h” (ἁμαρτία hamartia), smooth ἀ = no sound.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Accents</b></td><td>Acute ά, grave ὰ, circumflex ᾶ. Originally pitch; read them as stress. They can fall only on the last three syllables.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Iota subscript</b></td><td>ᾳ ῃ ῳ — a silent ι written under the vowel; it often marks the dative.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Punctuation</b></td><td>; is a question mark · · (raised dot) is a colon or semicolon.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Latin · beyond the letters</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Vowel length</b></td><td>Long vowels (marked with macrons in textbooks) take longer to say — and can change meaning: <i>mālum</i> apple, <i>malum</i> evil.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Diphthongs</b></td><td>ae aisle [church: “ay”] · au how · oe oil [“ay”] · ei eight · eu eh-oo · ui we</td></tr><tr><td><b>Stress rule</b></td><td>Two syllables: stress the first. Longer: stress the second-to-last if it is long, otherwise the third-to-last: <i>a-MĪ-cus</i>, <i>DO-mi-nus</i>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>I and V</b></td><td>I is “ee” or “y” (<i>iam</i> “yam”); V is “oo” or “w” (<i>vīnum</i> “wee-num”) [church: “v”].</td></tr><tr><td><b>Church Latin</b></td><td>c, g soften before e / i (<i>caelum</i> “chay-loom”); ti + vowel = “tsee.” Both systems are respectable; pick one and be consistent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>= The three-syllable window.</i></b> <i>In both languages, the accent never falls further back than the third syllable from the end.</i></p></figure>"}, {"title": "2 · The First Principle", "html": "<p class=\"jc-sub\"><i>endings, not order, carry the meaning</i></p>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Order decides · endings decide</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Sentence</th><th>Word order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>English · order decides</b></td><td>Dog bites man.</td><td>subject · verb · object</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Man bites dog.</td><td>subject · verb · object</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greek · endings decide</b></td><td>ὁ κύων δάκνει τὸν ἄνθρωπον.</td><td>subject · verb · object</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>τὸν ἄνθρωπον δάκνει ὁ κύων.</td><td>object · verb · subject</td></tr><tr><td><b>Latin · endings decide</b></td><td>Canis hominem mordet.</td><td>subject · object · verb</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Hominem canis mordet.</td><td>object · subject · verb</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<ul><li>English: <i>Swap the words and the meaning flips. English nouns barely change form.</i></li><li>Greek: <i>Both say “the dog bites the man.” The nominative (ὁ, -ων) marks the biter; the accusative (τόν, -ον) the bitten.</i></li><li>Latin: <i>Both say “the dog bites the man.” The order changes the emphasis, not who bites whom.</i></li></ul>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Why this matters</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nouns, adjectives, pronouns, and the article <b>decline</b>: their endings change for case, number, and gender.</td><td>Nouns, adjectives, and pronouns <b>decline</b> for case, number, and gender — there is no article to help.</td></tr><tr><td>Verbs <b>conjugate</b>: the ending tells you who acts. λύω = “I loose”; λύομεν = “we loose.” No pronoun needed.</td><td>Verbs <b>conjugate</b>: <i>amō</i> = “I love”; <i>amāmus</i> = “we love.” A pronoun (<i>ego</i>) adds emphasis.</td></tr><tr><td>Reading method: find the verb, then find its subject (nominative), then its object (accusative).</td><td>Reading method: the same. Word order is freer, but prose tends to end with the verb.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>= Same engine.</i></b> <i>Both are inflected languages. Learn the case system once and use it twice.</i></p></figure>"}, {"title": "3 · Nouns & Cases", "html": "<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The noun</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Cases</b></td><td>5: nominative, genitive, dative, accusative, vocative</td><td>6: nominative, genitive, dative, accusative, ablative, vocative (+ a locative relic: <i>Rōmae</i> “at Rome”)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Genders</b></td><td>3: masculine, feminine, neuter</td><td>3: masculine, feminine, neuter</td></tr><tr><td><b>Numbers</b></td><td>2: singular, plural (Attic’s dual has vanished in Koine)</td><td>2: singular, plural</td></tr><tr><td><b>Declensions</b></td><td>3 families of endings: 1st (mostly feminine, -α / -η), 2nd (-ος masc., -ον neut.), 3rd (consonant and vowel stems)</td><td>5 families: 1st (-a), 2nd (-us / -um), 3rd (consonant &amp; i-stems), 4th (-us, <i>manus</i>), 5th (-ēs, <i>rēs</i>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>≈ 5 cases vs. 6.</i></b> <i>Latin keeps an ablative. Greek hands its jobs to the genitive and dative.</i></p></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>What each case does</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Nominative (=)</b></td><td>Subject; predicate noun: ὁ λόγος</td><td>Subject; predicate noun: <i>verbum</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>Genitive (≈)</b></td><td>“of”: possession, description, part of a whole — <b>and “from”</b> (separation, source)</td><td>“of”: possession, description, part of a whole; object of some verbs</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dative (≈)</b></td><td>“to / for”: indirect object — <b>and “by, with, in”</b> (means, manner, place and time when)</td><td>“to / for”: indirect object, interest; possession: <i>mihi est liber</i> “I have a book”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Accusative (=)</b></td><td>Direct object; motion toward (εἰς); extent of time and space; subject of an infinitive</td><td>Direct object; motion toward (<i>ad</i>, <i>in</i>); extent of time and space; subject of an infinitive</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ablative (≠)</b></td><td>— none. Its jobs are split: “from” goes to the genitive; “by, with, in” to the dative.</td><td>“by, with, from, in”: means, manner, separation, place and time when; follows many prepositions</td></tr><tr><td><b>Vocative (=)</b></td><td>Direct address: κύριε “O Lord”</td><td>Direct address: <i>domine</i> “O Lord”</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<div class=\"jc-row\"><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Greek paradigms · endings in bold</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>ἀρχή <i>f · 1st</i></th><th>λόγος <i>m · 2nd</i></th><th>ἔργον <i>n · 2nd</i></th><th>σάρξ <i>f · 3rd</i></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>N</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ή</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ος</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ον</b></td><td>σάρξ</td></tr><tr><td><b>G</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ῆς</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ου</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ου</b></td><td>σαρκ<b>ός</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ῇ</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ῳ</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ῳ</b></td><td>σαρκ<b>ί</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ήν</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ον</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ον</b></td><td>σάρκ<b>α</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>V</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ή</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ε</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ον</b></td><td>σάρξ</td></tr><tr><td><b>N pl</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>αί</b></td><td>λόγ<b>οι</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>α</b></td><td>σάρκ<b>ες</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>G pl</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>ῶν</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ων</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>ων</b></td><td>σαρκ<b>ῶν</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>D pl</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>αῖς</b></td><td>λόγ<b>οις</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>οις</b></td><td>σαρξ<b>ί(ν)</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>A pl</b></td><td>ἀρχ<b>άς</b></td><td>λόγ<b>ους</b></td><td>ἔργ<b>α</b></td><td>σάρκ<b>ας</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>Plural vocative = nominative. Vocative singular of σάρξ = nominative.</i></p></figure><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Latin paradigms · endings in bold</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>puella <i>f · 1st</i></th><th>dominus <i>m · 2nd</i></th><th>verbum <i>n · 2nd</i></th><th>rēx <i>m · 3rd</i></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>N</b></td><td>puell<b>a</b></td><td>domin<b>us</b></td><td>verb<b>um</b></td><td>rēx</td></tr><tr><td><b>G</b></td><td>puell<b>ae</b></td><td>domin<b>ī</b></td><td>verb<b>ī</b></td><td>rēg<b>is</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>puell<b>ae</b></td><td>domin<b>ō</b></td><td>verb<b>ō</b></td><td>rēg<b>ī</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>puell<b>am</b></td><td>domin<b>um</b></td><td>verb<b>um</b></td><td>rēg<b>em</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Ab</b></td><td>puell<b>ā</b></td><td>domin<b>ō</b></td><td>verb<b>ō</b></td><td>rēg<b>e</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>V</b></td><td>puell<b>a</b></td><td>domin<b>e</b></td><td>verb<b>um</b></td><td>rēx</td></tr><tr><td><b>N pl</b></td><td>puell<b>ae</b></td><td>domin<b>ī</b></td><td>verb<b>a</b></td><td>rēg<b>ēs</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>G pl</b></td><td>puell<b>ārum</b></td><td>domin<b>ōrum</b></td><td>verb<b>ōrum</b></td><td>rēg<b>um</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>D pl</b></td><td>puell<b>īs</b></td><td>domin<b>īs</b></td><td>verb<b>īs</b></td><td>rēg<b>ibus</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>A pl</b></td><td>puell<b>ās</b></td><td>domin<b>ōs</b></td><td>verb<b>a</b></td><td>rēg<b>ēs</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Ab pl</b></td><td>puell<b>īs</b></td><td>domin<b>īs</b></td><td>verb<b>īs</b></td><td>rēg<b>ibus</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>4th: manus, manūs “hand” · 5th: rēs, reī “thing,” diēs “day.”</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>= 2nd declension twins.</i></b> <i>-ος -ον -ε ≈ -us -um -e. Same ancestor ending.</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>Neuter rule.</i></b> <i>Nominative = accusative; plural ends in -α / -a.</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>3rd declension.</i></b> <i>Find the stem in the genitive: σαρκ-ός, rēg-is.</i></p></figure></div>"}, {"title": "4 · Article, Adjectives, Pronouns", "html": "<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The article · “the” in 24 forms</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>m. sg</th><th>f. sg</th><th>n. sg</th><th>m. pl</th><th>f. pl</th><th>n. pl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>N</b></td><td>ὁ</td><td>ἡ</td><td>τό</td><td>οἱ</td><td>αἱ</td><td>τά</td></tr><tr><td><b>G</b></td><td>τοῦ</td><td>τῆς</td><td>τοῦ</td><td>τῶν</td><td>τῶν</td><td>τῶν</td></tr><tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>τῷ</td><td>τῇ</td><td>τῷ</td><td>τοῖς</td><td>ταῖς</td><td>τοῖς</td></tr><tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>τόν</td><td>τήν</td><td>τό</td><td>τούς</td><td>τάς</td><td>τά</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Attributive:</b> ὁ ἀγαθὸς λόγος = “the good word.”</li><li><b>Predicate:</b> ὁ λόγος ἀγαθός = “the word <i>is</i> good” — no verb needed.</li><li><b>Turns anything into a noun:</b> τὸ ἀγαθόν “the good”; οἱ ἐν οὐρανῷ “those in heaven.”</li><li>No word for “a”: λόγος alone = “a word.”</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>No articles · adjectives do the work</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>masc.</th><th>fem.</th><th>neut.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Greek</b></td><td>ἀγαθ<b>ός</b></td><td>ἀγαθ<b>ή</b></td><td>ἀγαθ<b>όν</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Latin</b></td><td>bon<b>us</b></td><td>bon<b>a</b></td><td>bon<b>um</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<ul><li><i>verbum bonum</i> = “a good word” or “the good word” — context decides.</li><li><i>verbum bonum est</i> = “the word is good” — Latin usually keeps the verb.</li><li>Adjectives as nouns: <i>bonī</i> “good men,” <i>bonum</i> “the good.”</li><li>Comparison: <i>fort-ior, fort-issimus</i> “braver, bravest” ≈ Greek σοφώ-τερος, σοφώ-τατος “wiser, wisest.”</li></ul>\n<p><b>≠ Article vs. none.</b> Greek marks “the”; Latin leaves it to context. Later, Latin <i>ille</i> “that” became the Romance articles: le, el, il.</p>\n<p><b>= Agreement.</b> Adjectives match their noun in gender, number, and case in both.</p>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Pronouns side by side</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>I</b></td><td>ἐγώ</td><td>ego</td><td><i>Both drop subject pronouns; using one adds emphasis.</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>you (one)</b></td><td>σύ</td><td>tū</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>we</b></td><td>ἡμεῖς</td><td>nōs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>you (all)</b></td><td>ὑμεῖς</td><td>vōs</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>he, she, it</b></td><td>αὐτός, -ή, -ό</td><td>is, ea, id</td><td><i>Greek αὐτός also means “-self” and, after the article, “same.”</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>-self</b></td><td>αὐτός</td><td>ipse</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>this</b></td><td>οὗτος, αὕτη, τοῦτο</td><td>hic, haec, hoc</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><b>that</b></td><td>ἐκεῖνος, -η, -ο</td><td>ille, illa, illud</td><td><i>ille later became le, el, il.</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>who, which (relative)</b></td><td>ὅς, ἥ, ὅ</td><td>quī, quae, quod</td><td><i>Agrees with its antecedent in gender and number; its case comes from its own clause.</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>who? what?</b></td><td>τίς, τί</td><td>quis, quid</td><td><i>Greek: the question word always keeps its acute (τίνος, not τινός); unaccented τις = “someone.”</i></td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>"}, {"title": "5 · Verbs", "html": "<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>The verb system</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Person (=)</b></td><td>1st, 2nd, 3rd — shown by the ending</td><td>1st, 2nd, 3rd — shown by the ending</td></tr><tr><td><b>Number (=)</b></td><td>Singular, plural (the dual is gone in Koine). A neuter plural subject often takes a singular verb.</td><td>Singular, plural</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tense (≠)</b></td><td>Present, imperfect, future, aorist, perfect, pluperfect. They mark <b>aspect</b> everywhere, and time only in the indicative.</td><td>Present, imperfect, future, perfect, pluperfect, future perfect. They mark <b>time</b> (and completion).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Voice (≈)</b></td><td>Active, <b>middle</b> (acting for or on oneself), passive. Deponents: middle form, active meaning — ἔρχομαι “I come.”</td><td>Active, passive. Deponents: passive form, active meaning — <i>loquor</i> “I speak.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mood (≈)</b></td><td>Indicative, subjunctive, imperative, and <b>optative</b> — rare in Koine, mostly wishes: μὴ γένοιτο “may it never be!”</td><td>Indicative, subjunctive, imperative. The subjunctive does the optative’s work: <i>utinam veniat</i> “if only he would come.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Greek asks “what kind of action?” · Aspect</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Kind of action</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Present · Imperfect</b></td><td><i>ongoing, in progress, repeated</i></td><td>λύω “I am loosing” · ἔλυον “I was loosing”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aorist</b></td><td><i>the action as a whole, a simple event</i></td><td>ἔλυσα “I loosed”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Perfect</b></td><td><i>completed, with results that still stand</i></td><td>λέλυκα “I have loosed (and it stays loosed)”</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Latin asks “when?” · Six tenses (timeline, left to right)</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Tense</th><th>Form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Past</b></td><td>Pluperfect</td><td>amāveram</td><td>“I had loved”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Past</b></td><td>Perfect</td><td>amāvī</td><td>“I loved / have loved”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Past</b></td><td>Imperfect (ongoing, in the past)</td><td>amābam</td><td>“I was loving”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Now</b></td><td>Present</td><td>amō</td><td>“I love”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Future</b></td><td>Future perfect</td><td>amāverō</td><td>“I will have loved”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Future</b></td><td>Future</td><td>amābō</td><td>“I will love”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>≠ Aspect vs. time.</i></b> <i>Outside the indicative, Greek tense forms show only aspect. Latin tenses place the action in time. The Latin perfect covers both the Greek aorist and the Greek perfect.</i></p></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Present active & “to be” (endings in bold)</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>λύω “I loose”</th><th>εἰμί “I am”</th><th>amō “I love”</th><th>sum “I am”</th><th>English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1 sg</b></td><td>λύ<b>ω</b></td><td>εἰμί</td><td>am<b>ō</b></td><td>sum</td><td>I</td></tr><tr><td><b>2 sg</b></td><td>λύ<b>εις</b></td><td>εἶ</td><td>amā<b>s</b></td><td>es</td><td>you</td></tr><tr><td><b>3 sg</b></td><td>λύ<b>ει</b></td><td>ἐστί(ν)</td><td>ama<b>t</b></td><td>est</td><td>he, she, it</td></tr><tr><td><b>1 pl</b></td><td>λύ<b>ομεν</b></td><td>ἐσμέν</td><td>amā<b>mus</b></td><td>sumus</td><td>we</td></tr><tr><td><b>2 pl</b></td><td>λύ<b>ετε</b></td><td>ἐστέ</td><td>amā<b>tis</b></td><td>estis</td><td>you all</td></tr><tr><td><b>3 pl</b></td><td>λύ<b>ουσι(ν)</b></td><td>εἰσί(ν)</td><td>ama<b>nt</b></td><td>sunt</td><td>they</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>= Same ancestral endings.</i></b> <i>-μεν ≈ -mus · -τε ≈ -tis · -ουσι ≈ -nt · ἐστί ≈ est. “is” in both comes from one root, *h₁es-.</i></p></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Building a Greek verb · front and back</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Front</th><th>Stem</th><th>Sign</th><th>Ending</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>ἐ (augment)</b></td><td>λύ</td><td>ο (vowel)</td><td>μεν</td><td><i>“we were loosing” · imperfect</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>ἐ (augment)</b></td><td>λύ</td><td>σα (tense)</td><td>μεν</td><td><i>“we loosed” · aorist</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>λε (redupl.)</b></td><td>λύ</td><td>κα (tense)</td><td>μεν</td><td><i>“we have loosed” · perfect</i></td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Greek · 6 principal parts</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>Future</th><th>Aorist act.</th><th>Perfect act.</th><th>Perf. mid./pass.</th><th>Aorist pass.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>λύω</b></td><td>λύσω</td><td>ἔλυσα</td><td>λέλυκα</td><td>λέλυμαι</td><td>ἐλύθην</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<div class=\"jc-row\"><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Building a Latin verb · the back only</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stem</th><th>Sign</th><th>Ending</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>amā</b></td><td>bā (tense)</td><td>mus</td><td><i>“we were loving” · imperfect</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>amā</b></td><td>vi (perfect)</td><td>mus</td><td><i>“we loved / have loved” · perfect</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>amā</b></td><td>ve·rā (pluperfect)</td><td>mus</td><td><i>“we had loved” · pluperfect</i></td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Latin · 4 principal parts</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Present</th><th>Infinitive</th><th>Perfect</th><th>Supine · participle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>amō</b></td><td>amāre</td><td>amāvī</td><td>amātum</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>≈ Stem + sign + ending.</i></b> <i>Both stack tense signs and personal endings onto a stem. Greek also marks the front: the augment ἐ- (past, indicative only) and reduplication (perfect).</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>Principal parts.</i></b> <i>Memorize them and you can build almost every form.</i></p></figure></div>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Greek · verb families & verbal nouns and adjectives</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>-ω verbs</b></td><td>λύω “loose” — the regular pattern</td></tr><tr><td><b>Contract</b></td><td>ἀγαπάω, ποιέω, πληρόω — the stem vowel merges with the ending</td></tr><tr><td><b>-μι verbs</b></td><td>δίδωμι give, τίθημι put, ἵστημι stand, εἰμί be</td></tr><tr><td><b>Irregular stems</b></td><td>ἔρχομαι / ἦλθον come · ὁράω / εἶδον see · λέγω / εἶπον say</td></tr><tr><td><b>Participles</b></td><td>λύων, λύσας, λελυκώς, λυόμενος, λυθείς, λελυμένος — every tense and voice, used everywhere: “the one who…,” “while…,” “after…,” “because…”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Infinitives</b></td><td>λύειν, λῦσαι, λελυκέναι, λυθῆναι; with the article: τὸ λύειν “the act of loosing”</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Latin · verb families & verbal nouns and adjectives</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>4 conjugations</b></td><td>1st <i>amāre</i> · 2nd <i>monēre</i> · 3rd <i>regere</i> (and -iō: <i>capere</i>) · 4th <i>audīre</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>Irregulars</b></td><td><i>sum</i> be, <i>possum</i> can, <i>eō</i> go, <i>ferō</i> carry, <i>volō</i> / <i>nōlō</i> / <i>mālō</i> want / not want / prefer</td></tr><tr><td><b>Deponents</b></td><td>Passive in form, active in meaning: <i>loquor</i> “I speak,” <i>sequor</i> “I follow”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Participles</b></td><td><i>amāns</i> loving · <i>amātus</i> having been loved · <i>amātūrus</i> about to love · <i>amandus</i> (gerundive) to be loved</td></tr><tr><td><b>Infinitives</b></td><td><i>amāre, amāvisse, amātūrus esse, amārī</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>Gerund, supine</b></td><td><i>ars amandī</i> “the art of loving” · <i>vēnit spectātum</i> “he came to watch”</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>≈ Old irregulars.</i></b> <i>-μι verbs and Latin sum are the same ancient type: εἰ-μί ≈ su-m.</i></p><p class=\"jc-cap\"><b><i>Participles.</i></b> <i>Greek has many and leans on them; Latin has four and often uses clauses instead.</i></p></figure>"}, {"title": "6 · Building Sentences", "html": "<p class=\"jc-sub\"><i>the syntax of years two to four</i></p>\n<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Constructions</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Construction</th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Negation (≈)</b></td><td>οὐ for facts (indicative); μή for everything else — commands, subjunctives, infinitives, most participles.</td><td><i>nōn</i> for statements; <i>nē</i> for prohibitions, purpose, wishes, and fears.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Yes-or-no questions (=)</b></td><td>οὐ expects “yes”; μή expects “no.” The question mark is;</td><td><i>nōnne</i> expects “yes”; <i>num</i> expects “no”; <i>-ne</i> is neutral.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prepositions (≈)</b></td><td>The case changes the meaning: διά + gen. “through,” + acc. “because of”; ἐν + dat. “in”; εἰς + acc. “into.”</td><td>The case changes the meaning: <i>in</i> + abl. “in,” + acc. “into”; <i>ad</i> + acc. “to”; <i>ex</i> + abl. “out of.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Purpose “in order to” (=)</b></td><td>ἵνα + subjunctive, Koine’s favorite: ἦλθεν ἵνα ἴδῃ “he came to see.”</td><td><i>ut</i> + subjunctive (<i>nē</i> if negative): <i>vēnit ut vidēret</i> “he came to see.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Result “so that” (≠)</b></td><td>ὥστε + infinitive (natural result) or indicative (actual result).</td><td><i>ut</i> + subjunctive — the same word as purpose, but negated with <i>ut nōn</i>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Indirect statement (≈)</b></td><td>ὅτι + indicative, much like English: λέγει ὅτι ἔρχεται “he says that he is coming.” Accusative + infinitive also occurs.</td><td>Accusative + infinitive: <i>dīcit sē venīre</i> “he says that he (himself) is coming.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Absolute phrases (≈)</b></td><td>Genitive absolute: αὐτοῦ λαλοῦντος “while he was speaking.”</td><td>Ablative absolute: <i>eō loquente</i> “while he was speaking”; <i>urbe captā</i> “the city having been taken.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Conditions (≈)</b></td><td>εἰ + indicative (assumed true) · ἐάν + subjunctive (future, likely) · εἰ + past indicative … ἄν (contrary to fact).</td><td><i>sī</i> + indicative (simple) · <i>sī</i> + present subjunctive (“should … would”) · <i>sī</i> + imperfect or pluperfect subjunctive (contrary to fact).</td></tr><tr><td><b>When, since, although (≈)</b></td><td>ὅτε “when,” ἐπεί “since,” καίπερ + participle “although” — or simply a participle.</td><td><i>cum</i> + subjunctive can mean “when,” “since,” or “although”; context decides.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sequence (≠)</b></td><td>Attic’s optional optative after past-tense verbs mostly disappears in Koine.</td><td>Sequence of tenses: a past main verb puts dependent subjunctives in past tenses — <i>vēnit ut vidēret</i>, not <i>videat</i>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Word order (≠)</b></td><td>Koine narrative often puts the verb early (καὶ ἐγένετο… “and it came to pass…”), partly Semitic style.</td><td>Classical prose likes the verb last: <i>Caesar pontem fēcit</i> “Caesar built a bridge.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>"}, {"title": "7 · Shared Words", "html": "<div class=\"jc-row\"><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Cognates · inherited from one ancestor</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th><th>English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>πατήρ</b></td><td>pater</td><td>father</td></tr><tr><td><b>μήτηρ</b></td><td>māter</td><td>mother</td></tr><tr><td><b>ὄνομα</b></td><td>nōmen</td><td>name</td></tr><tr><td><b>ἐγώ</b></td><td>ego</td><td>I</td></tr><tr><td><b>ἐστί</b></td><td>est</td><td>is</td></tr><tr><td><b>φέρω</b></td><td>ferō</td><td>I carry</td></tr><tr><td><b>γένος</b></td><td>genus</td><td>kind, birth</td></tr><tr><td><b>ἀγρός</b></td><td>ager</td><td>field</td></tr><tr><td><b>νύξ</b></td><td>nox</td><td>night</td></tr><tr><td><b>πούς</b></td><td>pēs</td><td>foot</td></tr><tr><td><b>ζυγόν</b></td><td>iugum</td><td>yoke</td></tr><tr><td><b>δόμος</b></td><td>domus</td><td>house</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Sound laws · predict the match</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Greek h- ↔ Latin s-</b></td><td>ἕξ / sex · ἑπτά / septem · ὑπέρ / super · ἅλς / sal · ἡμι- / sēmi-</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greek φ ↔ Latin f</b></td><td>φέρω / ferō “carry” · φηγός “oak” / fāgus “beech”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greek -ος, -ον ↔ Latin -us, -um</b></td><td>ζυγόν / iugum “yoke” · δόμος / domus “house”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Borrowed both ways</b></td><td>Into Latin: ecclēsia, angelus, baptizāre, philosophia. Into Koine: κεντυρίων, δηνάριον, λεγιών, πραιτώριον — centurion, denarius, legion, praetorium.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure></div>\n<div class=\"jc-row\"><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Counting to ten</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>εἷς</td><td>ūnus</td></tr><tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>δύο</td><td>duo</td></tr><tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>τρεῖς</td><td>trēs</td></tr><tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>τέσσαρες</td><td>quattuor</td></tr><tr><td><b>5</b></td><td>πέντε</td><td>quīnque</td></tr><tr><td><b>6</b></td><td>ἕξ</td><td>sex</td></tr><tr><td><b>7</b></td><td>ἑπτά</td><td>septem</td></tr><tr><td><b>8</b></td><td>ὀκτώ</td><td>octō</td></tr><tr><td><b>9</b></td><td>ἐννέα</td><td>novem</td></tr><tr><td><b>10</b></td><td>δέκα</td><td>decem</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"jc-cap\"><i>All cognate except “one”: εἷς comes from a root meaning “together” (Latin semel “once,” simplex), ūnus from another.</i></p></figure><figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>Everyday cognates in English</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>English</th><th>Latin</th><th>Greek</th><th>English words from them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>father</b></td><td><i>pater</i></td><td>πατήρ</td><td>paternal, patriarch</td></tr><tr><td><b>name</b></td><td><i>nōmen</i></td><td>ὄνομα</td><td>nominate, anonymous</td></tr><tr><td><b>foot</b></td><td><i>pēs</i></td><td>πούς</td><td>pedal, podiatry</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure></div>"}, {"title": "8 · A Four-Year Path", "html": "<figure class=\"jc-fig\"><figcaption>A four-year path</figcaption><div class=\"jc-tw\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Koine Greek</th><th>Latin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Year 1</b></td><td>Alphabet and sounds · the article · 1st and 2nd declensions · present, imperfect, future · prepositions · personal pronouns</td><td>Pronunciation · 1st and 2nd declensions · 1st and 2nd conjugations · all six indicative tenses · adjectives · start the 3rd declension</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><b>First reading: 1 John, John</b></td><td><b>First reading: adapted stories, Phaedrus’s fables</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Year 2</b></td><td>3rd declension · aorist and perfect · middle and passive · participles · subjunctive and imperative · contract and -μι verbs</td><td>3rd–5th declensions · 3rd and 4th conjugations · passive and deponents · participles and the ablative absolute · infinitives and indirect statement</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><b>Reading: Mark, then Luke</b></td><td><b>Reading: Caesar,</b> <i>Gallic War</i></td></tr><tr><td><b>Year 3</b></td><td>Syntax in depth: conditions, genitive absolute, uses of cases and infinitives, the optative · verbal aspect</td><td>The subjunctive in full: purpose, result, <i>cum</i>, indirect questions, conditions, sequence of tenses · gerund and gerundive</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><b>Reading: Acts, the Septuagint (Genesis), the Didache</b></td><td><b>Reading: Cicero’s speeches and letters, Catullus</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Year 4</b></td><td>Wide reading: Hebrews, the Septuagint, the Apostolic Fathers, Josephus, Epictetus · textual criticism · a bridge to Attic (Xenophon, Plato)</td><td>Virgil’s <i>Aeneid</i> · meter (dactylic hexameter) · Ovid, Horace, Livy · Church Latin: the Vulgate, Augustine, Aquinas</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><b>Goal: read with a lexicon, not a crib</b></td><td><b>Goal: read with a dictionary, not a translation</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure>"}, {"title": "In one line", "html": "<p class=\"jc-sub\"><i>Learn the cases once, use them twice. Learn the letters once, read them for a lifetime.</i></p>\n<p>Forms follow standard school paradigms · Pronunciations are approximate · Greek on the left, Latin on the right</p>"}]